window.docContents[159].resolve({"/Hyperbolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Transport-and-Classical-Waves--Nonlinear-Wave-Equations--Maximal-smooth-waves-in-three-dimensions":{"contents":"For smooth compact data in three dimensions, the reaction need only be\nsmooth and vanish at zero. Its derivatives may grow without bound as its\nargument grows. The construction uses a high Sobolev space to obtain the\nclassical field and then proves that smoothness persists up to its maximal\ntime. A finite lifespan imposes an amplitude obstruction.\n\nLet  with , and let\n. There are a lifespan\n and a field smooth on\n satisfyingEvery competing solution with these data that is  for \nand  up to  has . If , thenThus the displacement is unbounded on every terminal time strip.\n\nStart with the mild evolution in .\nSmooth composition is locally Lipschitz there. Sobolev embedding recovers\na classical field, and persistence at higher orders makes it smooth for\nsmooth compact data. Classical uniqueness identifies competing solutions\nwith this trajectory, so its maximal lifetime is also maximal among the\nstated classical competitors.If displacement were bounded on a terminal strip, the tame composition\nestimate would bound the source by an affine function of the Sobolev state\nnorm on that strip. Grönwall would then bound the full state and allow\ncontinuation past , contradicting maximality.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Hyperbolic Equations\u0009Transport and Classical Waves\u0009Nonlinear Wave Equations","header":"7.2.4.4. Maximal smooth waves in three dimensions","id":"/Hyperbolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Transport-and-Classical-Waves--Nonlinear-Wave-Equations--Maximal-smooth-waves-in-three-dimensions"},"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Extension___-Trace___-and-Embeddings--H___lder-Spaces--Canonical-Bounded-Operators":{"contents":"There are canonical norm-at-most-one continuous linear mapsand, for every ,The estimates are immediate from the uniform-norm term:Later proofs use these maps to extract uniform or pointwise information from\na Hölder-space element.\n\nIf  is a metric subspace, literal restriction defines a\ncontinuous linear contractionRestriction preserves every admissible Hölder constant, so neither the\nuniform norm nor the least Hölder seminorm can increase:No measurability or extension hypothesis is involved. This is ordinary\npointwise restriction of continuous representatives, not the restriction\noperator on Sobolev equivalence classes.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Sobolev Spaces and Weak Derivatives\u0009Extension, Trace, and Embeddings\u0009Hölder Spaces","header":"4.3.3.3. Canonical Bounded Operators","id":"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Extension___-Trace___-and-Embeddings--H___lder-Spaces--Canonical-Bounded-Operators"},"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/#PDEAtlas--Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation--Proof-producing-computation--From-input-to-theorem":{"contents":"After importing CAS.CAS, calculations are written in ordinary\nreal-scalar notation. The frontend reifies that notation internally, so users\ndo not have to construct a Verified.Expr by hand.\n\nClosed finite certificates have a simpler route. After its semantic routes refuse\na goal, bare cega can evaluate a closed decidable proposition in the kernel,\nsuch as a bound on every entry of a finite integer table. cega [fast] first\ntries compiled evaluation of that proposition, then a computable polynomial\ninterval enclosure. It restores the proof state and environment on refusal\nbefore falling back to kernel CEGA. Compiled successes additionally trust Lean's\ncompiler and native evaluation; their generated axioms reach downstream results.\nThe kernel build profile disables these native paths without changing the statements.\nExponential, logarithmic, and other proof-bearing analytic planner routes stay\nkernel checked. A closed finite certificate can still contain rational enclosures\nfor these functions: checking that finite data and proving its analytic meaning\nare separate steps.\n\nThe semantic theorem determines how we can use the answer. A derivative\nidentity lets us replace a term in a PDE operator. An enclosure\n for every  in a box can bound an error on that\nbox; it says nothing about points outside it. An antiderivative identity\nsupplies the derivative step of the fundamental theorem of calculus, whose\ninterval hypotheses must also hold.\n\nThe #cas command displays expressions and checked evidence together with\nany outstanding conditions or diagnostics. The theorem-facing tactics use\nthat evidence in ordinary Lean goals. If a closing tactic cannot justify\nthe requested conclusion, it fails and preserves the goal.\nThe SymLean manual gives the full command and tactic syntax,\ncoordinate-selection rules, side-condition contract, and limitations.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Verified Symbolic and Continuous Computation\u0009Proof-producing computation","header":"10.4.1. From input to theorem","id":"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/#PDEAtlas--Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation--Proof-producing-computation--From-input-to-theorem"}});